{"cross_mech":[],"family":{"alphafold_models":5432,"candidate_reasons":["pfam_previous_unknown_name"],"characterization_status":"","context_evidence_count":null,"cross_mech":{"example_proteins":0,"protein_traits_record":false,"records_by_mech":{},"scanned":false},"curation_status":"SEEDED","demotion_reasons":[],"description":"This family includes the toxic component HepT of a type II toxin-antitoxin (TA) system, which has RNase activity. These proteins contain a HEPN (higher eukaryotes and prokaryotes nucleotide-binding) domain and are neutralised through tri-AMPylation by the cognate antitoxin MntA, containing a MNT (minimal nucleotidyltransferase) domain [[cite:PUB00097221],[cite:PUB00097219],[cite:PUB00097220]]. HepT-MnA form an heterooctamer (at a 2:6 ratio), a rare organisation for this kind of TA systems. HepT dimerises and enables the formation of a deep cleft at the HEPN-domain interface, containing the RX4-6H motif (where X is any amino acid and the residue immediately after the conserved R is typically a polar amino acid) as the active site that functions as an RNA-cleaving RNase. This type II TA system regulates cell motility and confers plasmid stability [[cite:PUB00097219]]. Due to the prevalence of these HEPN/MNT modules in bacteria and archaea, it has been suggested that these TA systems may also play a role in the environmental adaptation to extreme habitats [[cite:PUB00097220]].","domain_architectures":17,"interpro_id":"IPR008201","known_evidence_count":null,"matches":7950,"name":"Ribonuclease HepT-like","partial_evidence_count":null,"pfam_id":"PF01934","proteins":7949,"proteomes":4926,"short_name":"HepT-like","source_url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/interpro/api/entry/pfam/PF01934","structures":11,"taxa":8359,"unknown_status":"EX_DUF"},"inputs":{"cross_mech":"cross-mech-duf-examples-2026-10-10","members":"pfam-uniprot-uniref90-2026-10-07","protein_examples":"duf-protein-examples-2026-10-13","worklist":"interpro-pfam-duf-2026-10-10"},"members":[],"metada